The solution is incorrect. The error is at n = 25: the lcm contains only a single factor of 5 per multiple of 5, and the highest power of 5 already in the lcm of 1–24 is 5¹ (from 5, 10, 15, 20 — each contributes only one factor of 5). The claim that "10·20 supplies 5²" is wrong because the lcm takes the maximum prime power, not products of arbitrary terms. So 25 does introduce 5², and it should be included.

The correct list is 11, 13, 16, 17, 19, 23, 25, 27, 29, and the sum is 155 + 25 = 180.

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 98, "correct_answer": 180}

The solution makes an error at n = 25. The claim that 25 divides a_24 because "10·20 carries 5²" is wrong: lcm(1,…,24) is 16·9·5·7·11·13·…, which contains only one factor of 5 (no 5² appears in any number ≤ 24). Since 25 = 5² introduces the new prime power 5², a_25 ≠ a_24, so 25 should be included.

Everything else checks out; the correct list is 11, 13, 16, 17, 19, 23, 25, 27, 29, whose sum is 180.

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 100, "correct_answer": 180}

The proposed solution overcounts. Subsets containing all three squares (1, 4, and 9) are counted once for each of the 3 possible square pairs, i.e., three times.

Correct count: choose exactly 2 squares and 3 non-squares: C(3,2)·C(12,3) = 3·220 = 660; or all 3 squares and 2 non-squares: C(3,3)·C(12,2) = 66. Total = 726.

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 100, "correct_answer": 726}

The proposed solution sets up the problem and finds the bounds for the positive root $r$ correctly ($8 \le r \le 41$). Note that any negative integer root $r'$ simply pairs with a positive root $r = -r' - 7$ to produce the exact same value of $n$, so counting the positive roots is sufficient to count the distinct values of $n$. 

However, there is a "fencepost error" in the final counting step. The number of integers from 8 to 41 inclusive is $41 - 8 + 1 = 34$, not $33$.

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 100, "correct_answer": 34}

